WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.491 --> 00:00:03.482
[Music]

00:00:04.446 --> 00:00:13.169
Each drawing is made from hundreds if not thousands of letters, numbers, punctuation marks, all puzzle pieced together.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.286
My name is James Cook.

00:00:16.286 --> 00:00:18.880
I am a&nbsp;typewriter artist.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.080
26 year old James Cook is&nbsp;an artist from Essex, England.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.510
But he doesn't carry a sketchbook and pencils...

00:00:34.707 --> 00:00:39.320
Instead he makes every one of his intricate drawings on a vintage typewriter.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.440
I'll feed a&nbsp;paper through the machine probably eight to nine times 

00:00:43.661 --> 00:00:47.617
collaging and overlaying lots and&nbsp;
lots of information.

00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:52.719
To recreate skin tones, I'll use the @ symbol because it has quite a&nbsp;large surface area.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:57.054
Brick work is underscores and capital I's to get the vertical lines.

00:00:57.054 --> 00:01:05.143
From&nbsp;a distance, it could quite easily be like a print or an illustration where it's only when the&nbsp;viewer gradually knees closer and closer to&nbsp;the image  

00:01:05.143 --> 00:01:09.420
that all of these individual letters,&nbsp;
numbers and punctuation marks are revealed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.833
Everyone is drawn to peoples' eyes so that's the&nbsp;hardest facial feature to get right.

00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:17.040
They have to be perfect.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:26.132
Something else I will do to add another&nbsp;
layer of complexity to the drawings is conceal hidden words or phrases for the viewer to find.

00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:30.141
How long does all this typing and tapping take?

00:01:30.245 --> 00:01:35.419
Something that's A4 sized could take a week. There's&nbsp;a picture of the London skyline that took two months.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.120
How many key strokes is that?

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.172
Obviously I don't count how many.

00:01:41.881 --> 00:01:47.097
I did&nbsp;actually try and download a piece of software which counted the number of times the hammers&nbsp;hit the page

00:01:47.097 --> 00:01:50.680
but it stopped working after fifty thousand, so I have no idea.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:57.349
James' favorite&nbsp;typewriters all have portable cases so he can pack them up and type outdoors.

00:01:57.744 --> 00:01:59.059
People give a funny look&nbsp;to begin with.

00:01:59.059 --> 00:02:04.020
They'll be like - 'what's this guy doing with this typewriter on his lap? Why is&nbsp;he writing a letter in the middle of London?'

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:11.460
But then as they edge closer, they suddenly&nbsp;realize 'oh no this, this is, he's making a drawing.'

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.381
And James' shelves are filling up.

00:02:14.462 --> 00:02:19.807
I've got&nbsp;about 63 typewriters and counting, and I've been very lucky because the general public&nbsp;have seen what I do with them.

00:02:19.807 --> 00:02:25.447
They see it as a way of giving these machines a second&nbsp;life, otherwise they would be sitting around&nbsp;collecting dust.

00:02:25.575 --> 00:02:27.337
I've become a collector by&nbsp;
accident.

00:02:27.441 --> 00:02:28.929
I didn't plan to have all of these.

00:02:28.929 --> 00:02:32.112
They all have their own different personalities to them.

00:02:32.112 --> 00:02:33.671
They all kind of work in different ways.

00:02:33.741 --> 00:02:35.506
They have different sounds, a different feeling&nbsp;behind the keys.

00:02:35.506 --> 00:02:37.086
There's a lot of tap tap tap.

00:02:37.086 --> 00:02:39.353
All these typewriters make a lot of noise.

00:02:39.353 --> 00:02:42.133
There's&nbsp;some technology that will never die.

00:02:42.309 --> 00:02:48.174
That's quite amazing to think that even though most people&nbsp;generally don't write with a typewriter anymore, they still have a purpose.

00:02:48.394 --> 00:02:50.742
Is it fair to say&nbsp;you really love typewriters?

00:02:50.823 --> 00:02:55.719
Uh, ooh, I'm not gonna, I'm not, I'm. Whoa.

00:02:55.719 --> 00:02:56.849
Do I say yes to that?

00:02:56.849 --> 00:03:00.000
I&nbsp;like drawing more than typewriters so...

